{"cluster_id": "C-2007", "topic": "GcRoundLogRecord uses a fresh round_id for", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["B026-021"], "canonical_claim": "`GcRoundLogRecord` uses a fresh `round_id` for every round attempt and stamps it on Start, Phase, and Finish rows; `round` is unsuitable because it is zero on Start, unavailable on non-leader rounds, and known only after a folding CAS.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-25-cas-followups-detector-introspection-s42-forceclaim.md#task-5-per-phase-row-plumbing-schema-record-correlator"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2008", "topic": "The fix must not assume holes", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["B026-069"], "canonical_claim": "The fix must not assume holes are page-shaped or trust a particular store, must retain Probe A and its `HEAD` verdict, and should close Probe A's two blind spots with the authoritative chain rather than widen its witness rule.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-26-list-incompleteness-investigation.md#fix-evidence"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2009", "topic": "For each ref log GC performs", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-355", "B027-005", "B027-007"], "canonical_claim": "For each ref log, GC performs one GET of the log body because the transaction must be read; for each edge it performs one HEAD and one GET of the manifest body because the reducer needs the blob list for both `-1` and `+1` edges.; The measured ref-intake cost is `logs + 2 \u00d7 edges` round trips at approximately 0.9 ms per trip, making the phase request-bound rather than CPU-bound.; GC's `fold_ref_intake` is linear in the ref-log backlog at 256 logs/s sustained; the flat cost is per S3 request (~0.92 ms), decomposing as one GET per log body plus one per emitted manifest edge, so round trips equal `logs + 2*edges`, and edges-per-log climbs from 1.54 to 3.73 as backlog grows.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-26-overnight-s42-and-gc-perf-study.md#part-b-why-does-a-gc-round-take-tens-of-minutes", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-26-overnight-s42-and-gc-perf-study.md#task-b2-hypothesis-unnecessary-work-is-any-request-avoidable-at-all", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-26-list-incompleteness-investigation.md#L375-L381"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2010", "topic": "parallel GC fetching", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["B027-010", "B034-101", "B042-033"], "canonical_claim": "Per-log GETs and per-edge manifest GETs are independent fetches; their application order matters, but fetching can be parallelized subject to the existing pool concurrency limit. GC read parallelism is safe because tables fold independently and log reads can be prefetched while prior logs are applied; the ordering constraint applies to folding, not fetching. Parallel GC fetching should be bounded by the number of in-flight manifest bodies and should leave application strictly ordered while fetching log and manifest bodies ahead of use.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/BACKLOG.md#duration-serial-answer", "docs/superpowers/cas/draft-fixes-20260726.md#p1-parallel-fetch", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-26-overnight-s42-and-gc-perf-study.md#task-b3-hypothesis-serial-where-it-could-be-parallel-is-the-fold-single-threaded"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2011", "topic": "The bounded walk fix freezes each", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["B027-154"], "canonical_claim": "The bounded-walk fix freezes each round's work set at the round-start LIST tail: if `tail_listed > cursor`, exact-walk only through `tail_listed`; if equal, skip the namespace; if below, skip and count the stale listing, with no new persistent state.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-a-streams.md#task-15"]}

